---
import Root from '#/layout/root.astro'

import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
import lms from '#/assets/lms.jpg'
---

<Root
  title="Case Study: Learning Management System"
  description="Case study of the re-engineering of a Learning Management System."
>
  <article class="prose flow">
    <h1>Case Study: Learning Management System</h1>
    <p class="text-lg">
      This case study details the re-engineering of a legacy Learning Management
      System, which I was lead developer and project manager for while working
      at Under One Cloud.
    </p>

    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow">
      <Image
        src={lms}
        alt="A blank notepad on a desk with some coffee."
        loading="eager"
        width={800}
        quality="high"
        layout="constrained"
      />
      <figcaption>
        This write-up currently does not have any screenshots of <a
          href="https://my.maguiretraining.co.uk"
          target="_blank"
        >
          the re-engineered LMS
        </a> for licensing reasons. I'm working on it!
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section class="prose__fullwidth with-sidebar">
      <div class="sidebar"><h2>Requirements</h2></div>
      <div class="flow">
        <p>
          Maguire Training were looking for a renewal of their legacy <abbr
            title="Learning Management System"
          >
            LMS
          </abbr>. It needed:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            A refreshed design with configurable theming for branded dashboards.
          </li>
          <li>Increased feedback from the interface.</li>
          <li>Improved speed and stability.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="prose__fullwidth with-sidebar">
      <div class="sidebar"><h2>Tools and Libraries</h2></div>
      <div>
        <ul role="list">
          <li>React</li>
          <li>Next.js (App Router)</li>
          <li>Prisma + MariaDB</li>
          <li>CSS Modules</li>
          <li>TypeScript</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <h2>Project Brief</h2>
    <p>
      When UOC took over maintenance of Maguire Training's LMS, it was an{' '}
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASP.NET_Web_Forms" target="_blank">
        ASP.NET Web Forms
      </a> application running a proprietary data framework. The application was functional,
      but the design and performance were lacking compared to other competitor products
      in the LMS ecosystem, and from a maintenance and feature development perspective
      the custom framework was largely undocumented.
    </p>
    <p>
      Together, Maguire Training and UOC planned to renew the LMS using a full
      stack approach, improving the back-end performance and the front-end user
      interface for both administration and customer accounts. I was the lead on
      the project working alongside another colleague, and served as primary
      point of contact with the client.
    </p>
    <h2>Tech Choices</h2>
    <p>
      We elected to base the new LMS platform around Next.js. As a two person
      team, keeping the codebase lean was key, and being able to write front-
      and back-end code in a single language (TypeScript) without needing to
      context switch improved our developer velocity.
    </p>
    <p>
      The legacy LMS relied on MySQL 5.7 for its database needs. We elected to
      move the new application to MariaDB. This would give us the benefit of
      improved performance and more modern database features, and also allow us
      to migrate existing data structures over if needed.
    </p>
    <p>
      The final major library leveraged in the project was Prisma, selected for
      both its <abbr title="Object-Relational Mapping">ORM</abbr> library and its
      mature database migration toolset.
    </p>
    <h2>User Interface</h2>
    <p>
      We built a component library using <a
        href="https://github.com/css-modules/css-modules"
        target="_blank"
      >
        CSS Modules
      </a>. These let us write CSS for different components and pages within the
      system with the added benefit of automatic locally scoped class names.
      Again, a choice driven by pragmatism given our small development team:
      this allowed us to avoid accidentally stepping on each other's namespaces.
    </p>
    <p>
      A key element of the new interface was that it could be customised from
      the admin side to provide different branded themes for different
      customers. We achieved this through designing multiple palettes using <a
        href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Cascading_variables/Using_custom_properties"
        target="_blank"
      >
        CSS Custom Properties
      </a> which were applied to elements using <a
        href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/How_to/Use_data_attributes"
        target="_blank"
      >
        data attributes
      </a>. These could be controlled from the server when the pages were
      rendered.
    </p>
    <h2>Performance</h2>
    <p>
      The new tech stack enabled us to use development patterns which improved
      performance across the board. An example of this is in the report
      generation section of the administration dashboard. The legacy report
      system gave no feedback to the user as to report generation progress, and
      caused performance issues for other users of the site due to excessive
      server resource utilisation. Generation of some reports took upwards of 30
      minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      In contrast, the reengineered LMS is performant enough to run each report
      within a minute at most, and the results can be requested in either CSV or
      PDF format. For larger, more computationally complex reports, using a
      React-based framework let us provide more informative user interfaces with
      real-time progress bars. We were also able to move tasks like PDF
      generation to the client side, reducing computational load on the server
      and therefore impact on overall site performance.
    </p>
    <h2>Outcomes</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://my.maguiretraining.co.uk" target="_blank">The LMS</a> has now
      been active for over a year. User count has increased, with multiple branded
      sites being created for Maguire's customers during that timeframe. Maguire contracted
      UOC for continued development of new features on the system after launch, and
      together we have improved the experience for both Maguire's employees and their
      customers.
    </p>
  </article>
</Root>
